 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity**: $O(1)$ per query point, requiring two SDF evaluations, a few arithmetic operations, and a power function.
-*   **Memory Complexity**: $O(1)$, no auxiliary data structures.
-*   **Strengths**: Local blending allows for modular composition of implicit primitives. It is compatible with sphere tracing [S2].
-*   **Limitations**: The method introduces gradient discontinuities at the boundary of the blend support (where $f_i \ge 1$). This makes it unsuitable for optimization-based surface extraction methods that require $C^1$ continuity. The blend support is fixed by the reformulation threshold (1.0 in SDF units), which may not match the desired visual blend width if the SDFs have different scales or if a different support radius is desired [S1].
-*   **When to use**: When rendering implicit surfaces via sphere tracing or geometric ray marching where gradient continuity is not strictly required for the intersection step.
-*   **When not to use**: When using root-finding algorithms for surface extraction or when smooth $C^1$ transitions are visually required.
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ per sample point, assuming $f_1$ and $f_2$ are $O(1)$. The cost is dominated by the exponentiation and root operations.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$. No auxiliary data structures are stored.
+*   **Strengths:** Local blending allows for efficient culling using bounding volumes [S1]. It preserves the distance property better than additive metaballs for sphere tracing [S2].
+*   **Limitations:** The gradient discontinuities at the boundary of the blend domain make it unsuitable for gradient-based optimization or root-finding algorithms [S1, S2]. It is primarily designed for sphere tracing (geometric ray intersection) [S1, S2].
+*   **Scaling:** Scales linearly with the number of blended primitives if extended to $N$ inputs, but the local nature means only nearby primitives need evaluation.
 
 ## Background
-The superelliptic blend, also known as the pseudonorm blend, was introduced by Rockwood and Owen in 1987 [S1, S2]. It is based on the pioneering work of Antonio Ricci (1974), who defined implicit surfaces using strictly non-negative functions equal to one [S1]. The method was popularized in the context of sphere tracing for rendering implicit surfaces [S1, S2]. It is a specific case of the more general superquadric blending techniques.
+The superelliptic blend was introduced by Rockwood and Owen in 1987 [S1, S2]. It is based on the pioneering work of Antonio Ricci in 1974, who defined implicit surfaces using strictly non-negative functions set to one [S1]. The method is also referred to as the "pseudonorm blend" because it computes a $p$-norm distance to the union of surfaces. It is widely used in sphere tracing for rendering implicit surfaces due to its compatibility with the Lipschitz property required for safe step sizes [S2].
 
